import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as d}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Identity and grants","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/identity-and-grants.md","filePath":"concepts/identity-and-grants.md","lastUpdated":1793730295000}'),s={name:"concepts/identity-and-grants.md"};function n(r,e,i,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="identity-and-grants" tabindex="-1">Identity and grants <a class="header-anchor" href="#identity-and-grants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Identity and grants&quot;">​</a></h1><p>An applet is not a name. It is a specific set of verified bytes, running in one frame, speaking over one port. Grants attach to that, and die with it.</p><h2 id="identity" tabindex="-1">Identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Identity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>kehto binds an applet&#39;s identity to two things, and kernl keeps both:</p><ol><li><strong>The verified artifact hash.</strong> <code>kernl.resolve</code> fetches the manifest event, verifies its signature, fetches the artifact bytes by SHA-256 and verifies them through kehto&#39;s <code>resolveNapplet</code> (<code>applets/resolve.ts</code>). The identity is <code>AppletIdentity</code>, <code>{ dTag, hash }</code>: <code>dTag</code> names the applet across versions, <code>hash</code> is kehto&#39;s <code>aggregateHash</code> and names this build. Cached bytes are re-hashed on every read. A gateway that serves different bytes produces a different hash, which is a different identity with no grants.</li><li><strong>The live message port.</strong> The frame sends exactly one <code>napplet.hello</code> with exactly one transferred <code>MessagePort</code>. The bridge binds the identity to that port. Everything after the handshake travels over the port, never over <code>window.postMessage</code>.</li></ol><p>The binding is revoked, the port closed and the iframe removed when the shell observes any of:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Observation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>window-message</code></td><td>A window-level message other than the one <code>napplet.hello</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>second-hello</code></td><td>A second <code>napplet.hello</code> from the same frame.</td></tr><tr><td><code>invalid-hello</code></td><td>A <code>hello</code> without exactly one transferred port.</td></tr><tr><td><code>second-load</code></td><td>The frame loaded a second document, by navigation or reload.</td></tr><tr><td><code>port-closed</code></td><td>The port closed or a message failed to deserialise.</td></tr><tr><td><code>host</code></td><td>The shell revoked the binding itself: the user closed the window (<code>handle.close()</code>), or the guard quarantined it, in which case <code>host.onRevoked</code> is told <code>quarantine</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A revoked window&#39;s identity can never be re-bound to the same iframe. Opening the applet again means a new frame, a new handshake and a fresh guard state. Grants, however, outlive the frame: they are keyed by identity, not by window.</p><h2 id="grants" tabindex="-1">Grants <a class="header-anchor" href="#grants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Grants&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A grant is something the user gave and can take back. kehto&#39;s ACL holds, per <code>dTag:hash</code>, a capability set, a blocked flag and a state quota. kernl&#39;s <code>createGrants</code> (<code>grants/grants.ts</code>) keeps its own record per identity, <code>{ dTag, hash, domains, blocked }</code>, at <code>kernl:grants:v1</code>, and mirrors it into the ACL on every change. Two consequences:</p><ul><li><strong>A new build is a new grant.</strong> When the applet&#39;s author publishes a new artifact, the hash changes and the grants for the old hash do not apply. The user approved specific code, not a name.</li><li><strong>Grants are per applet, never global.</strong> There is no &quot;allow all applets to publish&quot; switch. The user decides per <code>(dTag, hash)</code>, domain by domain.</li></ul><p>A grant is expressed in domains, not capabilities. <code>DOMAIN_CAPABILITIES</code> (<code>grants/domain-capabilities.ts</code>) says which kehto capabilities a domain needs (<code>relay</code> is <code>relay:read</code> and <code>relay:write</code>; <code>shell</code> and <code>inc</code> need none), so the user is asked about <code>relay</code>, and the ACL receives both capabilities. The table is on the <a href="./../packages/core#grants">core package page</a>.</p><h2 id="restrictive-posture" tabindex="-1">Restrictive posture <a class="header-anchor" href="#restrictive-posture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Restrictive posture&quot;">​</a></h2><p>kehto&#39;s <code>createRuntime</code> hardcodes the ACL default policy to <code>&#39;permissive&#39;</code>: a capability with no entry in the ACL store is allowed. kernl wants the opposite, and until the upstream gap in <a href="./../alpha-status#upstream-gaps">Alpha status</a> is closed it produces the restrictive posture around the runtime in three places:</p><ol><li><strong>Explicit ACL writes.</strong> <code>KernlGrants.ensure(identity)</code> runs inside <code>kernl.plan</code>, before any frame exists. It commits the applet&#39;s record, and a commit writes the complete capability set: every capability in kehto&#39;s <code>ALL_CAPABILITIES</code> is granted when <code>BASELINE_DOMAINS</code> or an allowed domain needs it and revoked otherwise, then the blocked flag is applied and the ACL persisted. The permissive default is never consulted for an applet kernl runs, because every capability has an entry.</li><li><strong>Environment narrowing.</strong> <code>createEnvironmentResolver(grants)</code> is the <code>ShellAdapter.capabilities.resolveEnvironment</code> hook. For each identity it returns the baseline plus the domains the user allowed, intersected with what the shell offers live; a blocked applet gets <code>shell</code> alone, enough to finish the handshake and nothing more. kehto intersects the result with its own availability set, so the hook can only remove. The applet&#39;s <code>shell.init</code> therefore advertises a subset.</li><li><strong>Runtime firewall rules.</strong> <code>kernl.mount</code> calls <code>applyRuntimeFirewallRules</code>, which translates the guard profile&#39;s per-class rates into kehto&#39;s runtime firewall through <code>RUNTIME_OP_CLASSES</code> so that over-budget envelopes are refused before dispatch. The policy it sets is <code>allow</code>; deny and block are the grants layer&#39;s job. <a href="./guard#where-enforcement-happens">Guard</a> has the translation table.</li></ol><p>The baseline is <code>BASELINE_DOMAINS</code>: <code>shell</code>, <code>inc</code>, <code>theme</code>, <code>identity</code> and <code>storage</code>. It is read-only and has no network: the handshake, in-shell messaging, the theme, the user&#39;s public key and profile, and scoped storage. <code>grants.revokeDomain</code> refuses a baseline domain with <code>denied:baseline-domain</code>; <code>grants.allowDomain</code> refuses a domain the shell does not offer with <code>invalid:domain</code>, and is a no-op for a baseline domain.</p><p>The practical effect for an applet: <code>requires</code> in the manifest is honoured as a question, not a demand. <code>MountPlan.missingRequired</code> lists the required domains the environment does not include, and the host decides what to do before mounting; the web shell&#39;s launcher shows the required and optional domains as choices and calls <code>grants.allowDomain</code> for the ones the user turns on before it calls <code>kernl.plan</code>.</p><h2 id="consent-for-destructive-writes" tabindex="-1">Consent for destructive writes <a class="header-anchor" href="#consent-for-destructive-writes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consent for destructive writes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Most writes an applet asks the shell to sign are additive: a note, a reaction, a comment. Some replace or erase the account&#39;s public record, and kernl asks before signing those. The check is kernl&#39;s, not kehto&#39;s: kehto&#39;s runtime has no consent step for destructive signing. <code>createKernl</code> builds one gate with <code>createSigningConsent</code> (<code>consent/handler.ts</code>) and puts both routes an applet&#39;s write can take through it:</p><ul><li><strong><code>relay.publish</code>.</strong> kehto signs through <code>ShellAdapter.auth.getSigner(windowId)</code>; the signer kernl returns for a window awaits the gate in <code>signEvent</code> before the account signs.</li><li><strong><code>outbox.publish</code>.</strong> kehto&#39;s outbox service signs without naming the window, so kernl gates <code>outbox.publish</code> in the service wrapper (<code>kernl-services.ts</code>) before the message reaches the service; a refusal is answered as <code>outbox.publish.result</code> with <code>ok: false</code>.</li></ul><p><code>isDestructiveKind</code> accepts every replaceable kind (0, 3, 10000–19999), because signing one overwrites the version before it; every addressable kind (30000–39999), for the same reason; kind 5, a deletion; and kind 62, a request to vanish. For those, the gate first looks up the current version: <code>lookupReplaceable</code> reads the <code>EventStore</code> and, on a miss, asks the user&#39;s outbox and discovery relays through the applesauce address loader, waiting at most <code>CURRENT_VERSION_TIMEOUT_MS</code> (4000 ms). It then runs <code>describeChange(draft, before, applet)</code> (<code>consent/describe-change.ts</code>):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Severity</th><th>What the user sees</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>0</code></td><td><code>destructive</code></td><td>&quot;Update your profile&quot;: one line per changed field, with the value before and after.</td></tr><tr><td><code>3</code></td><td><code>destructive</code></td><td>&quot;Change who you follow&quot;: follows added and removed, counted, the first three named by abbreviated key.</td></tr><tr><td><code>5</code></td><td><code>destructive</code></td><td>&quot;Delete N events&quot;: one line per event id (<code>e</code> tag), and one &quot;Delete every version of&quot; line per address (<code>a</code> tag).</td></tr><tr><td><code>62</code></td><td><code>destructive</code></td><td>&quot;Ask services to erase your account&quot;: everything the account published, and which services are asked.</td></tr><tr><td><code>10002</code></td><td><code>destructive</code></td><td>&quot;Change your connections list&quot;: relays added and removed with their read/write markers.</td></tr><tr><td>10000–10099, 30000–30099</td><td><code>destructive</code></td><td>&quot;Change the list …&quot;: entries added and removed.</td></tr><tr><td>any other replaceable or addressable kind</td><td><code>destructive</code></td><td>&quot;Replace your kind N event&quot;: tags added and removed, and &quot;Content: Replaced&quot; when the content changes, without copying it.</td></tr><tr><td>everything else</td><td><code>routine</code></td><td>No prompt; the event is signed without asking.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>When no current version is found, in the store or on the network within the timeout, a replaceable or addressable summary starts with &quot;Current version: could not be loaded; this replaces it entirely&quot; (<code>UNKNOWN_CURRENT_VERSION</code>), so the user is not shown a first version that seems to remove nothing.</p><p>The gate calls <code>host.presentConsent({ summary, resolve })</code> and waits. <em>Allow</em> signs the event. Deny or dismiss rejects with <code>denied:consent</code>: nothing is signed and nothing is published. The core never times a prompt out. A destructive write from a window kernl did not mount is refused with <code>denied:unknown-applet</code>, one while nobody is signed in with <code>denied:signed-out</code>, and a write whose kind is not a number with <code>invalid:event-kind</code>.</p><p>Three limits are deliberate:</p><ul><li><strong>Only applet windows are gated.</strong> The shell&#39;s own signing, which has no window id, goes through the account&#39;s signer directly.</li><li><strong>Answers are not remembered.</strong> Each destructive write asks again; <code>ConsentPrompt</code> has no &quot;always allow&quot; switch.</li><li><strong>The user never approves raw JSON.</strong> The unsigned draft (empty <code>id</code> and <code>sig</code>) is on <code>ConsentSummary.event</code> for an &quot;advanced&quot; disclosure.</li></ul><p>kehto&#39;s own <code>ConsentRequest</code> is a different thing: the runtime raises it, without an event, when a firewall policy is <code>prompt</code>. <code>createConsentHandler</code> is registered for it and refuses those without asking; a future request that carries an event, from a window the shell can name, would be described and put to the user. kernl&#39;s runtime rules set policy <code>allow</code>, so the firewall path is not expected.</p><p>Deletions are not rate-limited more tightly than other publishes before dispatch: kind 5 shares the <code>relay:write</code> budget in kehto&#39;s runtime firewall. The guard&#39;s separate <code>relay:publish:delete</code> bucket runs only in kernl&#39;s own focus firewall, which observes each envelope before dispatch but cannot hold it, so an overrun becomes a guard event (<a href="./guard#where-enforcement-happens">Guard</a>).</p><h2 id="what-the-applet-learns" tabindex="-1">What the applet learns <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-applet-learns" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the applet learns&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The applet learns its own narrowed environment through <code>shell.init</code>. It does not learn why a domain is missing, whether other applets have it, or what the user&#39;s other grants are. The shell never gives it <code>window.nostr</code>, a key, the <code>EventStore</code>, or plaintext the user did not ask the shell to show it.</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./guard">Guard</a>: the behavioural limits that apply once a grant exists.</li><li><a href="./../how-tos/grant-and-revoke">Grant and revoke</a>: the shell-side calls.</li><li><a href="./../policies/security-posture">Security posture</a>: the mount rules this page relies on.</li></ul>',32)])])}const g=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
